{include:`layouts/default.html.dtmpl`}
  {fill:content}
    <div class="py-4">
      <h1 class="h3 mb-4">Privacy</h1>
      {if:content.body}
        {var:content.body raw}
      {else}
        {comment}
          THE STUB SAYS THERE IS NO POLICY RATHER THAN PRETENDING TO BE ONE.

          A generated privacy policy is worse than none: it states commitments
          nobody made, about processing nobody has described, and a reader has no
          way to tell it from a real one. So this says the true thing and names
          who has to fix it.

          Same self-replacing shape as the front page: it lives in the `{else}`
          branch, so writing the page removes it with nothing to switch off.
        {endcomment}
        <p class="lead">This site has not published a privacy policy yet.</p>
        <p>
          The page exists so the cookie notice has somewhere honest to point. It is
          ordinary content — the operator of this site replaces it with their own
          policy, which is not something software can write for them.
        </p>
        <p class="text-secondary small mb-0">
          If you are that operator: this page is at <code>/privacy</code>, under
          <strong>Content</strong> in the admin.
        </p>
      {endif}
    </div>
  {endfill}
{endinclude}
